you're watching the daily climate show  on sky  news on today's programme a warning that  the uk  isn't prepared for extreme heat waves  which could become more frequent  due to climate change we hear about the  sand storms and severe wind battering  those living in displacement camps  in yemen and how low carbon concrete  could be the key to building a greener  [Music]  future  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions now think of heatwaves and you  might picture cool drinks in the shade  or a family barbecue  in the sun but the red cross is warning  there's a dangerous perception gap in  the uk when it comes to the public's  awareness of the risks  of extreme heat now the charity says the  impact of climate change  could mean heat-related deaths in the uk  triple in the next 30 years  around 7 000 each year but  25 percent of people don't think the uk  is hot enough  to be at risk from heat waves now that's  despite a record  2566 excess deaths  in england alone last summer from the  heat  the warning follows a recent report from  the climate change committee that  criticized the government for allowing  more than half a million  new homes to be built without features  to cope with higher temperatures  well a study published today in the  journal nature suggests extreme heat  events which break weather records by  large margins  will become more likely as a result of  climate change  so let's bring in dr helen hanlon senior  scientist at the met office  uh welcome to you first of all do you  agree that people in the uk are a little  naive  about the dangers of heat waves and  extreme heat  well currently we don't see we don't see  temperatures that often reaching to the  sorts of levels where you'll start to  see health impacts  these are projected to increase  dramatically with global warming though  um i have just done a study where i've  projected with the latest set of met  office climate projections  that if we reach two degrees of global  warming we could see a doubling  of the number of days where we will see  hot hot days  high enough to increase our risk due to  our health our health risk  but also um by four degrees if we  reached four degrees of global warming  this could be a four-fold increase so  yes this would be definitely  increasing um the frequency by which we  have um  days that pose a risk to our health from  extreme heat  and what is class as a heat wave here  so here um i have taken a hot day to be  a day where the temperatures exceed 25  degrees  and that means that the the daytime  temperatures exceed that could be up to  up to mid 30s by 2050s we are starting  to see in our climate projections  temperatures  exceeding 40 our current record  temperature in the uk  is 38.7 so this would be a considerable  increase in what we are used to  and would need absorb we would need to  adapt to  and clearly there's a battle on to try  to control the rise in temperatures but  if there are more heat waves on the  horizon what can be done to adapt to  them  so it's the things that you need to do  to sort of avoid um  heat exhaustion and things like staying  hydrated so if you're going out  take a bottle of water with you it's the  same as if you had a weather forecast  saying it was going to rain you take  your umbrella but in this case if it  says  extreme heat then you need to remember  to take your your water bottle with you  but there's  there's other things um to be concerned  about such as the most of our buildings  in the uk  are not built to withstand um high  temperatures  and keep us cool in those high  temperatures um so there is a  significant need for um changes to  building standards and also adapting  the homes that we currently have dr  helen helen thanks very much indeed  thank you very much let's take a look  now at some of the day's other climate  news and a town in worcestershire will  get  6.2 million pounds for a permanent flood  defense  after being washed out three years in a  row bewdley will have its temporary  barriers replaced to help protect homes  and businesses from future floods  a further 4.5 million pounds will be  invested in smaller projects within the  seven valley  to manage flooding in the long term  british vault says the construction of  its huge electric car  battery gigafactory in the uk is not far  away  a shovel in the ground ceremony took  place this morning on the site in  northumberland  where blythe power station used to be  it's hoped the gigafactory will produce  enough  lithium-ion batteries for 000 electric  cars a year  by 2027. and tourists and cornwall are  being encouraged to use their  phones to donate to local environmental  projects  qr codes have started appearing in  beauty spots across the region  as part of the love where you are  campaign people can scan the codes on  their phones to open up donation pages  for organizations that are working to  protect the environment and help  cornwall become  carbon neutral now we've talked a lot  recently about  extreme weather events such as floods  and wildfires but for many countries  sandstorms can also pose a lethal threat  in yemen harsh living conditions for  those in refugee camps  have been made even worse by the storms  which scientists say  could become more common and intense  because of climate change  well let's bring in abdul wasiya  muhammad who's oxfam's policy lead in  yemen  welcome to you tell us a little bit  about how bad these storms have been  lately and what it's like for people  living in those conditions  yeah thank you very much thank you for  having me and thank you for highlighting  the climate  issues in yemen just to start with the  the  climate change in yemen has not been an  issue that is being talked about a lot  due to the multiple crisis yemen has  already due to the conflict  which has started in 2015 this has  the the climate change issues have been  noticed in the past few  years especially across the the country  there have been devastating  impacts especially on vulnerable groups  like  the idp communities in across the  country  and in maribor i have been recently i  have seen devastating  impacts on idps where  they live already harsh conditions and  these  sandstones and dust stones have made it  worse for them  to to survive in their fragile  tents and and make shifts actually  one week into my arrival in yemen uh  there was a  an infant that has died due to the  collapse of a tent  uh due to these strong winds in in in  america  uh people in europe have been struggling  a lot  and so are across the country  about the strong winds and the  sandstorms and  all this dust have uh further worsened  their struggle well yes and how  concerned are you for the future and the  impact that  climate change could have in the years  ahead it's not just sandstorms is it  there is the risk of more floods and  droughts too  it is alarming especially uh because  people are already struggling with  accessing basic  services including health and water and  sanitation services  uh there are over one million idps  in in america in particular who are  struggling a  day on day-to-day basis to survive  and the strong winds the dust storms and  also the sandstorms have made it  have made their lives even worse  abdulbasia mohammed thanks very much  indeed  thank you now a new innovation could  mean  future emissions from the construction  industry don't have to be set in  precast concrete a uk team has developed  a process to reduce carbon emissions  from the building material  by up to 40 percent now we'll find out  more  about how they did it in a moment but  first here's why we need  the solution  [Music]  so  [Applause]  well joining me now is susan bernal  professor of structural materials at the  university of leeds welcome to you  first of all just how great is the  environmental impact of concrete  well thank you for having me this  evening and  it is vast and this is mainly due to the  high amount of concrete  that is needed to fulfill the demand for  infrastructure and buildings to  enable our cities to grow and develop  so what do you make of this new process  which could mean  that 40 percent fewer carbon emissions  are  emitted could this be the future  well i think this is an excellent  example that we can do more with  less by applying fundamental concepts  during the material selection  a process and by optimizing design which  can have a significant  impact in sustainability of structures  in my opinion the proposed approach is  really clever because it can be rapidly  implemented doesn't cause major changes  from current construction practices  that can enable us to achieve major  environmental savings in the years to  come  well yes so how optimistic are you for  the future that we can  find green alternatives to traditional  concrete  well this is my area of research so i  feel very optimistic about it  but over the past few years major cement  and concrete producers  in the uk and elsewhere have actively  worked  in the development and commercialization  of green alternatives  that are starting to become available in  the market  and i will say practitioners are also  becoming more aware  that we need to shift from using a  single material for every application  and instead we need to make use of any  potential option available  considering sustainability credentials  and are there cost implications of going  greener  in some instances there might be but in  the longer term  and with potential increase in carbon  taxes  and also extending longevity of these  sustainable materials  i think it could be a cost benefit  professor susan bernal thanks very much  indeed  thank you well that's everything from us  for today  next time bringing climate stories to  life through photography  you can find out more here tomorrow on  the daily climate show see you then  you

